#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Build the official-source research report from the frozen evidence inventory."""
import collections,json,pathlib
from acquire import ROOT,dump
STATE=ROOT.parent.parent
def main():
    cov=json.load(open(ROOT/'inventory-coverage.json'));hist=json.load(open(ROOT/'historical-audit.json'))
    refs={r['source_id']:r for r in json.load(open(ROOT/'source/history-original-references.json'))}
    ms=json.load(open(ROOT/'municipal-inspections.json'));pr=json.load(open(ROOT/'priority-investigations.json'))
    def u(s):return json.load(open(ROOT/'source'/(s+'.json')))['requested_url']
    def cite(s,label):return '['+label+']('+u(s)+')'
    text=f'''# New Hampshire official results: 2026 regular Class II U.S. Senate

Research snapshot: October 18, 2026. The deliverable inventories the state authority and **all 320 literal historical accounting leaves**, with a separate exact-key investigation for **each of the 143 frozen priority identities**. The priority records share 98 explicit municipal parent labels; these are not 143 separate clerk offices. Current municipal primary channels were successfully captured for 79 of those parents; 19 have direct-access gaps. All **23 original source rosters × 320 = 7,360 source-scoped records** remain available with original positions and raw fields. Neither counties nor municipal control totals replace those leaves. [Complete inventory](data/official-results-sources.csv), [143 investigations](data/results-sources/priority-investigations.json), [98 municipal reviews](data/results-sources/municipal-inspections.md), [coverage](data/results-sources/inventory-coverage.json).

No populated November 2026 Senate results feed, current result-unit/election/contest/candidate IDs, public refresh cadence or exact remaining-vote total was verified. Current SOS routes returned 403; those responses are retained, not interpreted as empty results. Static local documents and geographic viewers provide useful evidence within their own scopes. This is source research and reproducible historical extraction; it does not claim an implemented collector, current geographic approval, calibrated Needle or 2026 turnout forecast. {cite('sos-current-results','SOS current results channel')} ([retained state inspection](data/results-sources/state-inspection.json)).

**What is on the current general ballot.** Independently rendering the exact Farmington clerk-linked two-page original confirms page 1 says FARMINGTON / GENERAL ELECTION / NOVEMBER 3, 2026. United States Senator, vote for not more than one: **John E. Sununu — Republican; Edmond Laplante — Constitution; Chris Pappas — Democratic**, followed by write-in space. This is an actual general-election sample, not a primary candidate list. It establishes those visible ballot identities in Farmington; it supplies no public result IDs or complete statewide ballot-style crosswalk. [Clerk parent](https://www.farmington.nh.us/1206/Town-Clerk-Tax-Collector), [exact sample](https://www.farmington.nh.us/DocumentCenter/View/3121), [independent visual review and original pins](data/results-sources/current-ballot-review.json), [page 1 preview](data/results-sources/qa/farmington-ballot-page1.png).

**Authority and publication scope.** The moderator conducts the public local count and announces the result; the clerk certifies and forwards the local return; SOS examines and totals all constituent returns. Under the current statute, paper and electronic returns follow completion of the count, ordinarily reach SOS by 8 a.m. the following day unless SOS orders another time, and the local copy is open to inspection. The current law requires the clerk to post return copies within seven days. The separate moderator certificate/checklist/ballots forms generally have 48-hour requirements. None of these deadlines is a verified public web refresh interval. Federal certificates are issued by the governor under the recount/appeal conditions, rather than by an online percentage counter. {cite('rsa-659-chapter','RSA 659:63,71,73–75,81,84')}.

The state planning municipal directory is a discovery aid, not an election-authority crosswalk. Its Strafford label incorrectly links Stratford; that link was explicitly excluded, and the actual Strafford clerk channel was independently recovered. Names, URL slugs, county labels and synthetic percent-encoded source keys do not approve authority, current boundaries or numeric result IDs. Territorial, unincorporated and delegated authority remains unknown where primary evidence was not established. {cite('state-municipal-directory','State municipal directory')}, {cite('municipal-strafford-alternate1','actual Strafford clerk')}.

**Historical accounting and independent controls.** Independent original-byte extraction retained every workbook row/cell type or PDF page/line, then compared 34,880 native fields and 483 county/state controls with both finished first-wave workers. The entire descending order and 143-key threshold agree. Final turnout and partisanship pins are verified in [peer-final-verification.json](data/results-sources/peer-final-verification.json); the coordinator acceptance and priority contract remain read-only external authority. No reverse hash cycle or mutable maps manifest pin is introduced. [Extraction audit](data/results-sources/historical-audit.json), [validation](data/results-sources/history-validation.json), [full native records](data/results-sources/historical-native-records.jsonl.gz).

The ranking denominator is **826,189 reported numeric votes for four printed presidential tickets plus the generic Write-Ins column**: Harris/Walz 418,488; Trump/Vance 395,523; Oliver/ter Maat 4,425; Stein/Ware 3,680; generic Write-Ins 4,073. It is not a finding that all generic marks are valid named write-in votes. Under 4,482 and over 362 are separate fields; adding them yields the 831,033 presidential-accounting total also printed by the House Clerk, not a universal count of eligible voters or valid votes. The FEC independently prints 826,189 with scattered write-ins. NARA's certificate independently confirms the four printed ticket counts; repeated elector rows are overlapping slate observations, never extra votes. [SOS linked original]({refs['sos-2024-president-archived-original']['requested_url']}), [House Clerk 2024](https://clerk.house.gov/member_info/electionInfo/2024/statistics2024.pdf), [FEC original](https://www.fec.gov/resources/cms-content/documents/2024presgeresults.pdf), [NARA certificate](https://www.archives.gov/files/electoral-college/2024/ascertainment-new-hampshire.pdf), [independent controls and original positions](data/results-sources/independent-controls.json).

Top 15 = 185,659 / 826,189. The first 142 contribute 660,378; adding Tilton's 2,246 gives 662,624 at rank 143. Integer checks establish **660,378 × 5 < 826,189 × 4 ≤ 662,624 × 5**, so 143 is the exact minimum prefix reaching 80%. The other 177 records remain inventoried. These are historical source identities and weights, not November turnout, legal precincts or current reporting completeness. [Frozen ordered keys](data/coordinator-priority-keys.txt), [frozen contract](data/turnout/priority-set.json), [coordinator acceptance](data/coordinator-priority-acceptance.json).

2020 is the previous election for this Class II seat: Shaheen 450,778; Messner 326,229; O'Donnell 18,421; Scatter 486, total 795,914. The 2022 Senate race is the other, Class III seat; 2024 is presidential comparison data, with no Senate election. House Clerk 2020 independently agrees with the same-seat Senate counts. [House Clerk 2020](https://clerk.house.gov/member_info/electionInfo/2020/statistics2020.pdf), [SOS 2020 original]({refs['sos-2020-senate-archived-original']['requested_url']}).

All vintages are preserved. The archived 2024 index capture 20260917180704 links president_3.xls, ballots_cast_14.xls and checklist_7.xlsx. A later archive capture of unsuffixed president.xls contains older/different counts and does not supersede the linked _3 merely because its capture is later. In 2022 the linked _5 detail columns are Bolduc/Hassan while SUMMARY reverses their order; header-based mapping and the remediated PDF agree at Bolduc 275,631 / Hassan 332,490. Earlier _2 and House Clerk instead print 275,928 / 332,193. The opposing 297 changes leave the pair sum 608,121 and full total 620,975 unchanged. Release/amendment chronology and cause are unproved; no rescaling or preferred-vintage overwrite occurs. [2022 linked _5]({refs['sos-archived-2022-ge-us-senator-5-xls']['requested_url']}), [earlier _2]({refs['sos-archived-2022-ge-us-senator-2-xls']['requested_url']}), [remediated PDF]({refs['sos-archived-2022-ge-us-senator-remediated-1-pdf']['requested_url']}), [House Clerk 2022](https://clerk.house.gov/member_info/electionInfo/2022/statistics2022.pdf). [Archived 2024 index](https://web.archive.org/web/20260917180704id_/https://www.sos.nh.gov/2024-general-election-results). The full archived index chain and HTTP clocks are retained through [original references](data/results-sources/source/history-original-references.json).

Administrative fields stay separate. In ballots_14, Coos native/footer 17,511 conflicts with summary 17,510. Somersworth Ward 3 regular 1,119 + absentee 103 = 1,222 while the literal total is 1,221. The current-linked checklist gives Hopkinton 55 + 46 + 195 = 296 against literal total 3. The 2020 ballots sheet uses side-by-side A:D/G:J, statewide 554,282 regular + 260,217 absentee = 814,499; the separate checklist prints 1,119,232 and 75,611 new registrations. These are source-specific administrative measures, not candidate votes, final valid-vote universes or current geometry. Blanks, dashes and correction asterisks stay literal; a numeric sum is explicitly a sum of available numeric cells and never fills blanks with zero. [Ballots_14]({refs['2024-ge-ballots-cast-14-xls']['requested_url']}), [checklist_7]({refs['2024-ge-names-on-checklist-7-xlsx']['requested_url']}), [2020 ballots]({refs['2020-ge-ballots-cast-xls']['requested_url']}), [audited controls](data/results-sources/historical-audit.json).

**All 23 source scopes.** Each row below retains its own 320-record roster and complete original positions. County summaries, detail footers and municipal parents are controls. Literal cross-roster county/trimmed-label references are navigation only: 289 basis leaves match all 23 literal labels; 31 have fewer literal matches because of source labels/asterisks/abbreviations. Every unmatched row remains in its own complete source roster. No fuzzy or positional cross-year identity assignment is made.

|Source-native scope|Leaves|Original primary download/capture|Raw field families|
|---|---:|---|---|
'''
    for a in hist:
        sid=a['source_id'];r=refs[sid]
        text+=f"|`{sid}`|{a['native_rows']}|[original]({r['requested_url']})|"+', '.join(a['native_numeric_sums'])+'|\n'
    text+='''
**Observed local alternatives and schemas.** The linked public products actually inspected are mainly static HTML/PDF/XLS(X) publications. Civil-service web platforms, VotingWorks report branding and geographic ArcGIS/MapGeo viewers are not evidence of a public election-night result API. No endpoints were synthesized from vendor names or asset IDs. The full parent/href chain, literal href, HTML BASE, source character position, HTTP response and complete document text/page positions are retained in the source and derived inventories.

'''
    text+=f'''Portsmouth's local 2024 general matrix supplies four tickets × five wards and an overlapping city total. All 20 ward/candidate cells match SOS and each city total equals its five wards. Missing write-in/under/over fields are unavailable, not zeros. Londonderry's 2024 presidential panel supplies seven candidate/generic/under/over fields and scanned/manual/total columns: 16,050 + 299 = 16,349, also equal to the presidential seven-field sum. Scanned/manual are counting modes, not proven election-day/absentee methods. Adjacent offices instead show manual 265 and ballots 16,315; the mismatch is retained without extrapolation. Its report-created clock is Nov 6, 2024, 2:01:48 p.m. EST. A separate write-in adjudication report created 2:18:07 p.m. EST says 86 total / 0 not adjudicated; it overlaps the generic 86 and includes literal names/slogans, so it does not establish 86 valid named votes. {cite('sample-portsmouth-november-5-2024-state-general','Portsmouth original')}, {cite('municipal-londonderry-documents1','Londonderry tally')}, {cite('sample-londonderry-2024-general-election-write','write-in document')}, [independent local audits](data/results-sources/sample-audits.json).

Salem's September 8, 2026 Republican primary Senate panel has five polling-site columns (Woodbury 1, Woodbury 2, North Salem, Fisk, Barron), eight printed candidates plus write-in/over/under rows. All 55 site cells and 11 row totals reconcile to 456 + 557 + 759 + 520 + 518 = 2,810. This tests a real local schema and count scope; neither the primary's candidates nor these sites become November candidate IDs or a subdivision of the historical Salem row. {cite('municipal-salem-documents1','Salem primary original')}.

Manchester publishes separate primary return links for all 12 wards under one city clerk. Concord has separate results, polling locations/hours, map, street-list and ward-official channels. Keene links five ward general samples separately from primary official returns; Dover's current general page lists six wards. Rochester's six historical wards remain individually inventoried despite the current clerk access failure. Lebanon retains three historical ward identities. None of these source labels alone approves a November reporting-unit crosswalk. {cite('municipal-manchester-clerk-deep1','Manchester clerk')}, {cite('municipal-concord-pages1','Concord results')}, {cite('municipal-keene-clerk-deep1','Keene elections')}, {cite('municipal-dover-pages1','Dover general election')}, [individual ward records](data/results-sources/priority-investigations.json).

The current Berlin clerk page calls Precinct 1 former Wards 1 and 2, and Precinct 3 former Wards 3 and 4; the historical Berlin source remains one row. Merrimack publishes two polling locations, while Hudson publishes Ward One/Ward Two and a GIS link. Derry's recovered current election page links voting-district maps and polling information. These are real current local distinctions without any historical vote allocation. Maps-worker observations further distinguish map-native object IDs from result IDs; no mutable maps outputs are pinned here. {cite('municipal-berlin-elections1','Berlin precinct information')}, {cite('municipal-merrimack-pages2','Merrimack elections')}, {cite('municipal-hudson-pages2','Hudson polling')}, {cite('municipal-derry-election-detail','Derry elections')}.

Nashua's current /205 HTML lists Ward 1 at Nashua High School North and links View/3836. The maps worker independently retained that exact PDF; the visible map legend says Broad Street with an effective January 2026 / revised February 2026 footer, while underlying text also contains January 2022. This conflict is unresolved. The separate /866 direct 404 does not establish that /205 or its actual linked map failed. Portsmouth's current Ward 4 location notice is separately published. Current primary map reference is not approval of November boundaries or a source vote crosswalk. {cite('municipal-nashua-polling-current','Nashua current HTML')}, [Nashua exact linked map](https://www.nashuanh.gov/DocumentCenter/View/3836/City-Ward-Map-With-Detail-PDF), {cite('municipal-portsmouth-pages3','Portsmouth clerk notice')}.

Correction and access observations remain qualified: Northwood's actually linked official and unofficial 2026 primary PDFs both return 200 after correct HTML BASE resolution; earlier wrong-path 404 attempts were an extraction-resolver error and remain auditable. Gilford's correctly BASE-resolved linked PDF still returns 404. Haverhill labels a corrected document as fixing misspellings, which does not prove vote decreases. Claremont's literal /2021-election-results route links a 2023 tally; New Ipswich displays a future November 26, 2026 notice date at October retrieval; Rindge's published path includes elections-draft. These labels and clocks require content validation and do not establish revision cadence or live/test status. {cite('sample-northwood-resolved-v2','Northwood official')}, {cite('sample-northwood-unofficial-resolved-v2','Northwood unofficial')}, [resolution repair audit](data/results-sources/resolution-corrections.json), [municipal source-by-source findings](data/results-sources/municipal-inspections.md).

**Poll closing is local.** Explicit November notices supply these examples. They are not a complete statewide hours roster; municipal office hours, September primary and March town-election hours are excluded. RSA 21:36 establishes Eastern time and the first-Sunday-in-November end of daylight saving; November 3, 2026 is EST (UTC−5). Voters waiting at scheduled close retain applicable registration/voting rights. {cite('rsa-time-21-36','RSA 21:36')}, {cite('rsa-654-chapter','RSA 654:7-a')}, {cite('rsa-659-chapter','RSA 659:12')}, [literal hours evidence and conversions](data/results-sources/poll-hours-evidence.json).

|Current notice|November 3 local hours|Polling scope|Closing UTC|
|---|---|---|---|
'''
    for h in json.load(open(ROOT/'poll-hours-evidence.json'))['local_hours']:
        text+=f"|[primary]({h['url']})|{h['opening_local']}–{h['closing_local']} EST|{h['polling_place_scope']}|{h['closing_utc']}|\n"
    text+=f'''
**Winner, write-ins and ties.** RSA 659:81 uses the highest vote count for the single office, not a majority threshold. A candidate must finish strictly above every other candidate; a two-candidate tie is not a win, and Laplante and valid write-in candidates cannot be dropped from the legal comparison. RSA 659:64/65 govern intent and defective ballots. Under 659:64-a/67, a write-in for a printed candidate is incorporated into that candidate's total, with one vote for the same person on one ballot. Under 659:73 the return separately reports other named write-in recipients and their sum, excluding votes already assigned to printed candidates. A generic historical mark count cannot substitute for that adjudicated named universe. A tie for largest vote for U.S. Senate triggers another election called by the governor with advice of the council under 660:25–26, repeated if necessary. {cite('rsa-659-chapter','RSA 659')}, {cite('rsa-660-chapter','RSA 660:25–26')}.

**Ordinary in-person and absentee scope.** Qualified voters must meet age, citizenship and local domicile requirements; election-day registration is available subject to qualification verification. The current statutory text and the court orders below must be read together. New Hampshire does not provide general no-excuse early voting. RSA 657:1 provides excuse-based absentee eligibility, including specified absence, religious, disability and employment/care reasons and qualified winter-storm circumstances. Current 657:17-c requires an absentee applicant's qualifying photo-ID copy, notarized signature, or in-person qualifying-ID verification before issuance. A qualifying delivery agent is governed by 657:17, not an arbitrary bearer. Ordinary completed absentee ballots must reach the clerk by **5 p.m. election day**, rather than merely be postmarked; 657:22 specifically excepts emergency workers under 657:21-a,V and disabled voters appearing under 659:20-a. Those exceptions are not a general late-arrival reservoir. {cite('rsa-654-chapter','RSA 654')}, {cite('rsa-657-chapter','RSA 657')}, {cite('rsa-659-chapter','RSA 659:20-a')}, {cite('lebanon-current-absentee','current Lebanon absentee guidance')}.

RSA 659:49 ordinarily starts absentee processing at 1 p.m. or a properly noticed earlier time at least two hours after opening; processing is not authority to publish a final count before polls close. Timely later receipts must still be processed. The separately signed absentee certificate and ballot/checklist/reconciliation forms measure administrative scope; the individual absentee-status lookup is not a public candidate-results or aggregate remaining-vote feed. No current November public UOCAVA late-arrival tail, general post-election cure pool, provisional-ballot count or exact outstanding quantity was verified. This is an explicit operational gap, not a claim all such categories equal zero. {cite('rsa-659-chapter','RSA 659:49–53')}, {cite('rsa-657-chapter','RSA 657:22–26')}, {cite('sos-current-uocava','SOS uniformed/overseas channel; direct access failed')}.

**Court-order limits as of the captured October 18 docket.** The First Circuit's September 4 order in 26-1740 denies a stay of the May 28 injunction concerning HB 1569's removal of qualified-voter and challenged-voter affidavits; the order expressly covers the September primary and November 3 general. This restores specified affidavit mechanisms, not every former affidavit-ballot procedure or a waiver of all qualifications. {cite('court-sep4-order','First Circuit original order')}.

The October 5 order in Coalition for Open Democracy (appendix PDF page 67 / printed 66a) leaves HB 323's removal of student ID from expressly valid ID in place, while narrowly enjoining April 23 guidance that prohibits or discourages considering those student IDs with other evidence under the specified identity-verification provisions. It does not make a student ID automatically sufficient by itself or change ballot printing. The district court denied a stay October 7; the First Circuit denied the renewed stay October 14 (PDF pages 74–75 / 73a–74a), with Judge Dunlap stating he would grant it. Supreme Court 26A512 was filed October 15; the captured docket requests a response by 11 a.m. EDT October 19 and lists no disposition. Later action requires rechecking the primary docket; a pending application is not itself a stay. {cite('court-scotus-appendix','Primary Supreme Court appendix containing orders')}, {cite('court-scotus-docket','Supreme Court docket')}.

Absentee signature procedure needs separate qualification: Saucedo, 2018 DNH 160, permanently enjoined the then-numbered RSA 659:50,III signature-matching process on due-process grounds. The current amended section contains signature comparison with verification exceptions; the old order cannot be ignored or silently converted into a new seven-day cure rule. This research did not recover a comprehensive current 2026 clerk cure/signature manual resolving every operational step, and does not invent a provisional or post-election cure universe. {cite('court-saucedo-order','Saucedo original order')}, {cite('rsa-659-chapter','current RSA 659:50–53')}, [exact statute positions and order scopes](data/results-sources/rule-evidence.json).

**Recount, canvass and certification.** RSA 660:1 permits a candidate's written recount application by the Friday after election (November 6 for this election) when the specified difference is below 20% of total ballots cast in the constituent towns; fees vary under 660:2. This is a request-based rule, not an automatic recount threshold. The process must begin by the Wednesday following the request deadline under 660:4; visual ballot inspection, protests, declaration and appeal provisions follow in 660:3–6. SOS canvass waits for all constituent returns under 659:81; the governor's Senate certificate follows 659:84's recount/appeal expiration or written-waiver conditions. An election-night announcement, full website geography coverage and a certified final result are different statuses. {cite('rsa-660-chapter','RSA 660:1–6')}, {cite('rsa-659-chapter','RSA 659:81/84')}.

**143 exact-key investigations.** Every row below points to a complete source-key record, basis raw fields/position, every exact literal cross-roster reference and the shared municipal review. Shared authority is reused explicitly; wards retain distinct identities and any actual published ward links. The synthetic keys have no state/source approval as current numeric IDs. The [CSV](data/official-results-sources.csv) retains the other 177 leaves with discovery-only scope, including unresolved territories.

|Rank|Exact historical source key|Literal native label|Explicit parent / current evidence|
|---:|---|---|---|
'''
    for r in pr:
        ar=r['authority_inspection'];s=ar['selected_current_channel'];url=s['url'] if s else ar['indexed_primary_alternative'];status='captured' if s else 'direct access gap'
        text+=f"|{r['priority_rank']}|`{r['source_geography_key']}`|{r['native_source_record']['native_label']}|[{r['explicit_municipal_parent_label']}: {status}]({url})|\n"
    text+='''
**Municipal coverage gaps and retained alternatives.** The 19 direct-current gaps are '''+', '.join(cov['direct_current_access_gaps'])+'''. Their exact requests, statuses, curl failures, indexed primary alternatives and archive-query outcomes remain in the individual records. Successful bounded 2026 archived primary captures were retained for Enfield, Hillsborough, Kingston, Lee and North Hampton, each with its own capture clock. Archived office content does not verify current results or supersede current court orders. No successful capture from a bounded archive query is a coverage statement about that query, not proof that a municipality has no official site or archive. [Full 98-parent evidence table](data/results-sources/municipal-inspections.md), [raw inspection records](data/results-sources/municipal-inspections.json).

**Use on election night.** Follow the actual SOS and clerk publication chains, validate the election date/office/general-versus-primary status, printed candidates and any numeric IDs before selecting a payload. Retain each snapshot, source clock and status; compare overlapping local/state counts without summing them. A decrease must remain a signed revision with its literal source version and explanation if supplied, rather than be forced monotonic or treated as newly uncounted votes. No observed snapshot series establishes actual decreases or a live cadence here. Reporting-unit coverage is not ballot completeness; stale timestamps, a future display date, a test calendar or an API-shaped geographic service must not be treated as current certified vote data. Volunteer/newspaper reports and projections in the separate news assignment remain distinct from moderator/clerk returns and SOS canvass/certification.

**Reproduction and bounds.** [SCHEMA](data/results-sources/source/SCHEMA.md) specifies field types, unavailable/null/blank handling, source positions, source-scoped identity, acquisition storage and commands. [reproduce.py](data/results-sources/reproduce.py) performs the offline original-byte extraction, independent controls, local arithmetic, first-wave pin comparison, inventory/report build and validation; it does not make network requests. [acquire_all.py](data/results-sources/acquire_all.py) can reproduce the observed URL requests into a separate destination without overwriting this snapshot. Every retained HTTP response keeps exact requested/final URLs, UTC retrieval, full headers/redirects/status, original/stored hashes and byte counts. Local gzip is lossless; an HTTP content-encoding layer, if present, remains identifiable in the original entity bytes. All owned objects are strictly below 40,000,000 bytes; originals reused from peers are read-only exact-hash references. No cache/dependency directories or huge duplicate originals are retained. [Offline validation](data/results-sources/validation.json), [complete owned asset manifest](data/results-sources/asset-manifest.json).

Material gaps remain precise: current November candidate/result IDs and publicly exposed schema/test/completion flags; current authoritative reporting-unit crosswalk and territorial/delegated clerk scope; named/valid historical write-in allocation; historical certification/amendment chronology including the opposing 2022 changes; current comprehensive cure/signature instructions and future court actions; full statewide November polling-hours coverage; actual live refresh behavior, outstanding ballots and count completion. These gaps are not filled by historical ballots, registration, map object IDs or media percentages.
'''
    (STATE/'official-results.md').write_text(text)
    print('Built official-results.md: all23 scopes,143 exact keys, primary citations and qualified operational gaps')
if __name__=='__main__':main()
